1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:02,000
Pay a toll.

2
00:00:02,001 --> 00:00:04,000
Grab some cash.

3
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:07,000
Pick up your lunch and your laundry.

4
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:11,000
Then say your wedding vows from the comfort of your car.

5
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:14,000
In the fast lane of life, there's a portal up ahead

6
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:17,000
where you can do all of these things and many more

7
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:19,000
without unbuckling your seatbelt.

8
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:23,000
Now, drive-thru on Modern Marvels.

9
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
What can I get for you?

10
00:00:44,060 --> 00:00:48,180
Yes, I'd like quarter ponder with cheese meal.

11
00:00:48,440 --> 00:00:49,680
Do you have a Big Mac extra value?

12
00:00:49,800 --> 00:00:51,400
A Happy Meal cheeseburger.

13
00:00:55,440 --> 00:00:59,380
Lunch shift at a McDonald's restaurant in La Puente, California.

14
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:06,680
rush hour. This state-of-the-art quick-serve restaurant with its double-lane

15
00:01:06,681 --> 00:01:13,440
drive-through system will serve over 200 orders between 11 and 2.

16
00:01:13,441 --> 00:01:22,080
In an industry where every six seconds of customer wait time saved equals 1% of

17
00:01:22,081 --> 00:01:24,080
market share.

18
00:01:24,081 --> 00:01:26,080
$11.08.

19
00:01:26,081 --> 00:01:27,080
$11.08.

20
00:01:27,081 --> 00:01:29,080
Thanks so much.

21
00:01:29,081 --> 00:01:32,080
Time is money.

22
00:01:32,081 --> 00:01:37,080
Inside the restaurant, computerized ordering systems and wireless communication tools help

23
00:01:37,081 --> 00:01:41,080
employees shave seconds from each transaction.

24
00:01:41,081 --> 00:01:47,080
Outside, customer visual displays speed up the ordering process.

25
00:01:47,081 --> 00:01:55,940
Fast-track in-car transponders enable customers to electronically bill their order to a credit card, allowing them to totally bypass the pay window.

26
00:01:58,280 --> 00:02:07,340
In a society dominated by the automobile and obsessed with speed, the drive-thru window has taken on a significant role in our nation's economy.

27
00:02:08,340 --> 00:02:11,500
Though fast food is still the king of drive-thru commerce,

28
00:02:11,880 --> 00:02:15,560
more and more businesses are turning to the so-called hole-in-the-wall

29
00:02:15,561 --> 00:02:17,600
to increase market share in sales.

30
00:02:23,880 --> 00:02:30,180
Tobacco shops, liquor stores, you name it, we get orders for all kinds of strange places.

31
00:02:30,880 --> 00:02:33,180
Will you take Robert for your husband?

32
00:02:34,360 --> 00:02:35,060
I will.

33
00:02:35,061 --> 00:02:41,060
Drive-thru business continues to grow in many cases to represent 50 to 60 percent of our business now.

34
00:02:41,700 --> 00:02:47,160
The drive-thru and the speed of service aspect, the convenience aspect, has become embedded in the culture.

35
00:02:47,960 --> 00:02:50,720
The drive-thru promises us speed and convenience.

36
00:02:51,300 --> 00:02:59,680
But at one time or another, most of us have found ourselves sitting in a long, motionless line of idling machines and wondering, how did we get here?

37
00:03:00,320 --> 00:03:02,720
Well, folks, we got here by car.

38
00:03:04,820 --> 00:03:12,760
In the early 1900s, the automobile was considered a high-priced toy for the rich racing set,

39
00:03:13,280 --> 00:03:17,520
until automotive pioneers like Henry Ford, using assembly line mass production,

40
00:03:18,060 --> 00:03:20,840
brought the price of the car within reach of the average family.

41
00:03:23,840 --> 00:03:29,220
For the first time, the house, the homestead, is not the center of leisure for the family.

42
00:03:29,221 --> 00:03:40,040
The family has a new toy, if you like, a new tool, that individual family members could go out of the town, to the countryside, to different areas.

43
00:03:41,500 --> 00:03:43,380
Now, the real race was on.

44
00:03:44,660 --> 00:03:50,820
For the first time in history, the masses were set free from the limitations of horse, steam, and rail.

45
00:03:51,640 --> 00:03:57,720
The car broadened our range of travel, but just as importantly, it delivered a new dimension to our experience.

46
00:03:57,721 --> 00:04:04,720
speed, speed on a personal, private level.

47
00:04:04,721 --> 00:04:08,720
The automobiles taught us to think in miles per hour rather than miles per day.

48
00:04:08,721 --> 00:04:14,720
But with all its potential power, this new phenomenon was dependent on a smelly petroleum by-product,

49
00:04:14,721 --> 00:04:18,720
without which it was just another shiny stationary object.

50
00:04:18,721 --> 00:04:25,720
Gasoline, petrol, push water, go juice.

51
00:04:25,721 --> 00:04:30,360
And although the term drive-through wouldn't enter the American lexicon for another 50 years,

52
00:04:31,080 --> 00:04:33,720
the gasoline service station was the original.

53
00:04:35,480 --> 00:04:38,760
It utilized all phases of modern drive-through transaction.

54
00:04:39,640 --> 00:04:44,280
Pull in, place an order, receive the order, pay for the order, drive out.

55
00:04:46,040 --> 00:04:48,440
It took a while for all this to happen smoothly.

56
00:04:49,880 --> 00:04:55,320
Actually, at the very beginning, when the 1890s, owners, the very few owners of cars,

57
00:04:55,321 --> 00:05:01,600
had to go to the edge of the town to certain gas distribution terminals and get the gasoline

58
00:05:01,601 --> 00:05:06,780
in a can, bring it to their automobile and put it in the tank, which was quite inconvenient

59
00:05:06,781 --> 00:05:08,060
and sometimes quite dangerous.

60
00:05:08,820 --> 00:05:13,920
Of course, what helped in this was the invention of the curbside pump, that gasoline could

61
00:05:13,921 --> 00:05:16,060
be transferred to the automobile via a hose.

62
00:05:17,420 --> 00:05:23,440
In 1913, the Gulf Corporation built the first drive-in service station in Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania.

63
00:05:23,441 --> 00:05:29,440
The brick pagoda-style structure sat on a raised island, flanked by crude hand-cranked pumps.

64
00:05:31,440 --> 00:05:37,740
By 1916, 200 oil companies were offering roadside service along the nation's highways.

65
00:05:38,900 --> 00:05:43,940
Customers would pump gas by hand while attendants figured out the charges with pen and paper.

66
00:05:45,800 --> 00:05:51,420
The 1920s brought the luxury of an electronic device called the VisiGage pump.

67
00:05:52,080 --> 00:05:55,960
A tall glass-domed dispenser that offered two octane grades of fuel.

68
00:05:57,040 --> 00:06:00,500
The addition of pump attendants brought in the era of full service.

69
00:06:01,420 --> 00:06:06,500
This pampering allowed the driver to refill his or her tank without leaving the driver's seat.

70
00:06:07,160 --> 00:06:09,220
The first true drive-thru had been born.

71
00:06:11,660 --> 00:06:15,900
Marketing tenants of the first gas stations were the same as the drive-thrus of today.

72
00:06:17,120 --> 00:06:21,060
Towering signage with long-distance impact and a recognizable logo.

73
00:06:21,420 --> 00:06:27,020
The shopping experience prior to the automobile was really a main street where you would walk

74
00:06:27,021 --> 00:06:30,840
down and purvey your goods through a window.

75
00:06:30,841 --> 00:06:36,180
With the automobile, all of a sudden you had people going 35 miles an hour down a highway.

76
00:06:36,181 --> 00:06:38,600
And how do you get people to stop at your business?

77
00:06:38,601 --> 00:06:41,040
Well certainly one way to do that is signage.

78
00:06:41,041 --> 00:06:45,180
So you would have gas stations, you would have billboards, you would have buildings

79
00:06:45,181 --> 00:06:49,540
that started to become signs.

80
00:06:49,541 --> 00:06:54,520
The next 40 years saw a rapid-fire succession of technical innovation designed to increase

81
00:06:54,521 --> 00:06:56,540
speed and convenience.

82
00:06:56,541 --> 00:07:04,040
Credit cards, digital displays, and self-service pumps helped raise the gas pump's speed limit.

83
00:07:04,041 --> 00:07:09,140
Someone once told me that buying gas ranked lower in terms of the consumer preference

84
00:07:09,141 --> 00:07:10,640
than having a root canal.

85
00:07:10,641 --> 00:07:14,420
I don't know whether that's true or not, but why do people put off buying gas?

86
00:07:14,421 --> 00:07:19,040
I think because they've got better things to do.

87
00:07:19,041 --> 00:07:24,000
In 1983, the POS, or point of sale system, was introduced by Shell Oil Company.

88
00:07:24,680 --> 00:07:27,740
The credit card reader was incorporated into the gas pump itself.

89
00:07:28,240 --> 00:07:30,060
Pump, swipe and go was the slogan.

90
00:07:30,860 --> 00:07:33,540
Surely the maximum speed for buying gas had been reached.

91
00:07:35,140 --> 00:07:36,100
Not quite.

92
00:07:36,840 --> 00:07:40,920
In the 1990s, companies like Transcor and Texas Instruments

93
00:07:40,921 --> 00:07:44,740
introduced a technology that would break the cash speed barrier.

94
00:07:44,741 --> 00:07:47,520
It was called RFID.

95
00:07:47,521 --> 00:07:51,655
RFID is simply radio frequency identification.

96
00:07:51,656 --> 00:07:55,520
I mean, simply put, it's just like a radio.

97
00:07:56,520 --> 00:08:04,940
The RFID, or SmartPass system, uses a radio sensor to identify a transponder inside the car, or a cell phone.

98
00:08:06,240 --> 00:08:11,360
Once the transaction is complete, it is electronically billed to the user's credit card account.

99
00:08:12,660 --> 00:08:16,980
Campaigns such as Mobile's Gas & Go also used RFID technology.

100
00:08:17,520 --> 00:08:27,240
Texas Instruments worked with Vexxon Mobile some years ago to apply it to the payment method at a gas station.

101
00:08:27,241 --> 00:08:40,309
And that's to provide the customer the convenience and the security and the speed of being able to use an RFID tag instead of paying by cash or by a traditional credit

102
00:08:40,310 --> 00:08:41,010
card.

103
00:08:43,040 --> 00:08:47,020
Once our tanks were full of gas, we were free to head for the open road.

104
00:08:47,520 --> 00:08:48,364
such as it was.

105
00:08:48,365 --> 00:08:52,300
At the turn of the century, most roads in America were leveled beds of

106
00:08:52,301 --> 00:08:54,606
packed earth covered with gravel.

107
00:08:54,607 --> 00:08:58,520
Wet weather often turned early motoring into an off-road

108
00:08:58,521 --> 00:09:06,302
experience. The first paved highway was laid near Detroit in 1908.

109
00:09:06,303 --> 00:09:08,660
In 1921, the federal

110
00:09:08,661 --> 00:09:11,340
government passed the Federal Highway Act.

111
00:09:11,341 --> 00:09:14,020
Ribbons of asphalt and concrete flowed out

112
00:09:14,021 --> 00:09:20,084
from the cities and spawned a new kind of living environment called suburbs.

113
00:09:20,085 --> 00:09:21,840
Multi-lane interstates

114
00:09:21,841 --> 00:09:28,640
spread from coast to coast, and with them came another drive-through, the toll booth.

115
00:09:28,641 --> 00:09:29,341
Toll

116
00:09:29,342 --> 00:09:32,197
roads probably date back to the beginnings of commerce.

117
00:09:32,198 --> 00:09:34,080
London Bridge had tolls as early

118
00:09:34,081 --> 00:09:36,385
as 1286. The concept was simple.

119
00:09:36,386 --> 00:09:40,780
Let the users of the road or bridge pay for its construction.

120
00:09:40,781 --> 00:09:45,860
Early toll roads were called turnpikes.

121
00:09:45,861 --> 00:09:50,500
The name derived from a wooden pole or pike that would block the road until the toll was

122
00:09:50,501 --> 00:09:54,540
paid, then turned to allow the traveler to pass.

123
00:09:54,541 --> 00:10:01,980
In 1935, the first major American toll road, the Pennsylvania Turnpike, was built connecting

124
00:10:01,981 --> 00:10:05,060
Pennsylvania with Ohio and New Jersey.

125
00:10:05,061 --> 00:10:09,500
It was a financial and engineering success and spawned imitation across the country.

126
00:10:10,340 --> 00:10:16,180
The years from 1945 to 1956 saw rapid growth in toll roads and bridges.

127
00:10:16,900 --> 00:10:23,440
But soon, the ever-rising numbers of cars began to choke the toll plazas and clog the on-ramps.

128
00:10:24,140 --> 00:10:28,080
For instance, the toll is 25 cents and somebody hands over a dollar bill.

129
00:10:28,081 --> 00:10:31,240
So you've got to find the change and hand that change over.

130
00:10:31,241 --> 00:10:34,840
and literally when there are peak travel times,

131
00:10:34,980 --> 00:10:37,860
when there are thousands of people, then it all accumulates.

132
00:10:39,700 --> 00:10:42,860
One attempt to standardize toll money in the form of tokens

133
00:10:42,861 --> 00:10:44,720
helped speed things up somewhat.

134
00:10:45,720 --> 00:10:47,780
But still, the lines grew.

135
00:10:48,840 --> 00:10:50,980
Some attendants were replaced with catch baskets

136
00:10:50,981 --> 00:10:53,860
that used gravity to funnel the coins down into coin counters.

137
00:10:54,700 --> 00:10:58,320
But poor aim and incorrect amounts still snarled the lines.

138
00:10:58,321 --> 00:11:06,720
Fortunately, in the 1990s, the same technology that had sped up fuel delivery came to the

139
00:11:06,721 --> 00:11:12,700
rescue of the beleaguered tolls, RFID, the SmartPass.

140
00:11:12,701 --> 00:11:17,160
Improvements to the RFID technology now allowed cars to be scanned and billed without even

141
00:11:17,161 --> 00:11:20,040
stopping.

142
00:11:20,041 --> 00:11:23,820
Drivers could pay their tolls at 50 miles an hour.

143
00:11:23,821 --> 00:11:33,000
The number of vehicles moving through the plazas rose from 180 per hour to 720 per hour.

144
00:11:33,001 --> 00:11:38,260
Faster and faster, the gas stations fed the cars and the tolls fed the highways.

145
00:11:38,261 --> 00:11:43,800
But who was feeding the drivers and passengers?

146
00:11:43,801 --> 00:11:48,380
By 1920, there were 8 million cars on the American highway.

147
00:11:48,381 --> 00:11:50,700
Not all of this travel was related to work.

148
00:11:50,701 --> 00:11:57,060
automobile had created a new pastime, motoring, touring, Sunday driving. As it

149
00:11:57,061 --> 00:12:00,840
became evident that people enjoyed spending time in their cars, it was only

150
00:12:00,841 --> 00:12:05,540
a matter of time before someone reasoned that they might like to eat there too.

151
00:12:05,541 --> 00:12:10,200
Though it is widely assumed that this phenomenon began in California, it

152
00:12:10,201 --> 00:12:16,380
actually happened in Texas. In 1921, the first drive-in in the world was open,

153
00:12:16,381 --> 00:12:17,300
That was the pig stand.

154
00:12:18,100 --> 00:12:23,160
And the idea came from J.G. Kirby, who was a mercantile in Dallas.

155
00:12:23,161 --> 00:12:29,140
And he approached Dr. Reuben Jackson, who was a prominent physician in Dallas,

156
00:12:29,940 --> 00:12:33,460
with the idea of opening up a drive-in restaurant.

157
00:12:34,340 --> 00:12:38,380
And the idea was to serve people exclusively in their cars,

158
00:12:39,220 --> 00:12:43,560
not have any inside service, have waiters to come out to the car.

159
00:12:43,561 --> 00:12:46,520
Dr. Jackson had the capital to fund the project.

160
00:12:47,120 --> 00:12:47,820
Well, Mr.

161
00:12:47,821 --> 00:12:52,500
Kirby, he was famous for his quote of saying that people with cars are so lazy

162
00:12:52,501 --> 00:12:54,500
that they don't want to get out of them to go eat.

163
00:12:55,220 --> 00:12:57,360
And that prophecy proved to be very true.

164
00:13:00,440 --> 00:13:03,280
Recreational driving had spawned recreational dining.

165
00:13:04,060 --> 00:13:06,480
The drive-ins created a kind of roadside theater.

166
00:13:07,260 --> 00:13:09,100
The main performers were waiters.

167
00:13:09,740 --> 00:13:12,820
They hired young boys to be the waiters.

168
00:13:13,560 --> 00:13:15,320
13, 14 years old.

169
00:13:16,060 --> 00:13:22,520
And they developed a game where when they would see the old Model Ts turning in toward the curb,

170
00:13:23,060 --> 00:13:28,600
they would race out, and whoever got on that running board of the old Model T, that was their customer.

171
00:13:29,020 --> 00:13:31,060
So it became a real competitive thing.

172
00:13:31,800 --> 00:13:36,560
And that's where the term car hop originated, because they hopped on the running board.

173
00:13:36,561 --> 00:13:44,260
The 1930s saw a neon explosion of drive-ins across the sunbelts of Florida, Texas and

174
00:13:44,261 --> 00:13:44,967
California.

175
00:13:44,968 --> 00:13:49,720
Signage and architecture became more and more outrageous in its attempt to

176
00:13:49,721 --> 00:13:51,880
yank hungry motorists off the highway.

177
00:13:51,881 --> 00:13:57,760
So you have this whole phenomena of this kind of California crazy pop architecture

178
00:13:57,761 --> 00:14:01,560
phenomena where buildings all of a sudden start to look like the objects that they were

179
00:14:01,561 --> 00:14:04,063
selling. So giant ice cream cones would appear.

180
00:14:04,064 --> 00:14:06,300
And then it rolled into giant pumpkins and

181
00:14:06,301 --> 00:14:11,400
giant pigs and giant dogs, and there was all kinds of business that came through that.

182
00:14:11,540 --> 00:14:16,660
The whole idea of advertising, you know, changed with the advent of the car and how you got

183
00:14:16,661 --> 00:14:17,660
people into your business.

184
00:14:18,720 --> 00:14:23,480
By the 1940s, drive-in restaurant competition was growing fierce.

185
00:14:24,740 --> 00:14:28,500
Drive-in owners fought for market share using any gimmick they could think of.

186
00:14:29,060 --> 00:14:32,480
One of the first major changes was to add sex appeal.

187
00:14:32,481 --> 00:14:39,480
They discovered that women had obviously much more appeal to be doing this kind of activity.

188
00:14:39,481 --> 00:14:45,480
So then the owners of some of these restaurants started to costume the carhops in various get-ups.

189
00:14:45,481 --> 00:14:50,480
So you might go to one restaurant that was themed, say, in Mount Vernon kind of motif.

190
00:14:50,481 --> 00:14:53,480
One would come out in hooskirts and the other one was just like a man as a butler,

191
00:14:53,481 --> 00:14:58,480
with this kind of southern, antibalum feel to it.

192
00:14:58,481 --> 00:15:04,200
Business boomed as the drive-ins reproved the old adage, sex sells.

193
00:15:05,060 --> 00:15:14,300
Down in Texas at Civil's drive-in, the owners there put their waitresses in very abbreviated costumes made out of satin and shorts.

194
00:15:15,480 --> 00:15:19,460
Immediately there was an uproar by people saying, now you've really gone too far.

195
00:15:19,540 --> 00:15:27,440
There was a lot of protest, but it became such a phenomenal Life magazine featured one of the carhops on the cover that this now became the standard.

196
00:15:27,441 --> 00:15:33,500
So they had these large plumed hats that look like drum majorettes, abbreviated costumes.

197
00:15:33,760 --> 00:15:38,320
Having a sexy dame feed you was pretty appealing in that 40s period.

198
00:15:39,580 --> 00:15:44,580
By the late 1940s, the drive-in concept was beginning to show signs of stress.

199
00:15:45,260 --> 00:15:46,840
And there were a lot of problems with drive-ins.

200
00:15:47,340 --> 00:15:50,980
They had a lot of theft of plates and silverware, which drove them nuts.

201
00:15:51,400 --> 00:15:57,140
You had all of the fry cooks fraternizing with the socializing with the carhops.

202
00:15:57,440 --> 00:15:59,620
and they oversaturated the market.

203
00:15:59,780 --> 00:16:02,260
There was only so much that this niche could carry.

204
00:16:03,100 --> 00:16:05,360
So you start to see, you know,

205
00:16:05,420 --> 00:16:07,580
the whole structure of this restaurant industry changing

206
00:16:07,581 --> 00:16:09,120
and its relationship to the car.

207
00:16:09,500 --> 00:16:11,680
Teenagers, in fact, were really one of the major parts

208
00:16:11,681 --> 00:16:13,020
of killing off drive-ins.

209
00:16:13,160 --> 00:16:14,160
They would play their radios,

210
00:16:14,200 --> 00:16:15,800
and they would order Coke and French fries

211
00:16:15,801 --> 00:16:18,480
because you had, you know, 15, 20, 30 spaces

212
00:16:18,481 --> 00:16:21,780
occupied by a bunch of rowdy teenagers who wouldn't leave.

213
00:16:21,960 --> 00:16:23,100
They scared away the family,

214
00:16:23,200 --> 00:16:25,560
which was the bread and butter of these drive-in restaurants.

215
00:16:25,561 --> 00:16:33,020
The industry needed a new system to remedy the problems and to get family business back into the restaurants.

216
00:16:33,880 --> 00:16:36,460
It found the answer in San Bernardino, California.

217
00:16:37,300 --> 00:16:40,160
It was the brainchild of a couple of brothers from New England.

218
00:16:40,720 --> 00:16:43,640
Their names were Maurice and Richard McDonald.

219
00:16:44,720 --> 00:16:50,820
The McDonald brothers had a drive-in very much like all the other drive-ins that existed in California in the early 40s.

220
00:16:51,440 --> 00:16:53,800
It was a unique shape. It was an octagon.

221
00:16:53,801 --> 00:16:56,160
But it also had all these carhops.

222
00:16:57,800 --> 00:17:04,220
In 1948, the McDonald brothers decided to take a gamble on a completely new concept.

223
00:17:04,780 --> 00:17:09,680
They did something that few business operators dream of doing.

224
00:17:09,840 --> 00:17:15,180
They took a drive-in that was making $100,000 a year for them, net.

225
00:17:15,700 --> 00:17:18,580
They totally shut down their restaurant.

226
00:17:18,581 --> 00:17:24,180
They took the menu down from 35 items down to 9 items.

227
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:27,440
They got rid of the carhops.

228
00:17:27,720 --> 00:17:30,360
They got rid of the silverware and the flatware.

229
00:17:30,900 --> 00:17:36,400
Three months later, they reopened with a restaurant that no one had seen before.

230
00:17:36,680 --> 00:17:37,380
No one.

231
00:17:38,160 --> 00:17:43,620
This new restaurant was a self-serve drive-in that turned the customers into carhops.

232
00:17:43,621 --> 00:17:52,540
It was essentially a walk-up window that promised a limited menu of fresh and tasty, inexpensive food served at extreme speed.

233
00:17:53,280 --> 00:17:58,980
Everything was built on speed, and that was a whole different concept.

234
00:17:59,140 --> 00:18:02,200
They called this concept their speedy service system.

235
00:18:02,940 --> 00:18:08,240
They could turn around an order in as fast as 15 seconds.

236
00:18:08,241 --> 00:18:14,120
You could walk up to the counter, order a hamburger, order a milkshake, order a fries.

237
00:18:14,680 --> 00:18:16,660
They're all American meal, as they called it.

238
00:18:17,180 --> 00:18:19,760
And get out of there in 15 seconds.

239
00:18:20,020 --> 00:18:22,460
That was totally new.

240
00:18:23,060 --> 00:18:32,000
By 1952, from this little hamburger stand, these guys were doing $350,000 in revenue a year.

241
00:18:32,120 --> 00:18:34,420
That was unheard of.

242
00:18:34,421 --> 00:18:41,420
As word spread about this new speedy system, San Bernardino, California became a mecca for

243
00:18:41,421 --> 00:18:44,420
restaurant owners looking to increase speed of service.

244
00:18:44,421 --> 00:18:49,780
Everybody was going to San Bernardino in the food service industry to figure out what these

245
00:18:49,781 --> 00:18:54,420
brothers had done, and they all started to copy it.

246
00:18:54,421 --> 00:18:55,121
Glenn G.

247
00:18:55,122 --> 00:19:00,100
Bell, Jr., a San Bernardino native who patronized McDonald's regularly, applied

248
00:19:00,101 --> 00:19:04,100
the speedy service system to a Mexican food menu.

249
00:19:04,101 --> 00:19:08,540
His restaurant chain would later be named Taco Bell.

250
00:19:08,541 --> 00:19:09,642
In 1953, Keith G.

251
00:19:09,643 --> 00:19:14,180
Kramer visited the McDonald Brothers restaurant, then returned home to

252
00:19:14,181 --> 00:19:16,360
Daytona Beach, Florida.

253
00:19:16,361 --> 00:19:23,300
The restaurant he opened, Instaburger, would later become Burger King.

254
00:19:23,301 --> 00:19:29,160
A year later, a milkshake mixer salesman named Ray Kroc visited the McDonald Brothers restaurant

255
00:19:29,161 --> 00:19:33,700
to find out why such a small operation was ordering so many of his multi-mixers.

256
00:19:36,580 --> 00:19:39,500
Upon seeing the speedy service system in action,

257
00:19:40,180 --> 00:19:42,680
Kroc realized he had walked into a gold mine.

258
00:19:43,580 --> 00:19:46,120
He persuaded the McDonald brothers to sign a contract,

259
00:19:46,600 --> 00:19:49,960
giving him the sole rights to franchise their operation across the country.

260
00:19:50,360 --> 00:19:53,620
The brothers would receive 0.5% of all sales.

261
00:19:53,621 --> 00:20:01,020
On April 15, 1955, Kroc opened the first McDonald's franchise in Des Plaines, Illinois.

262
00:20:02,720 --> 00:20:06,620
Five years later, there were 200 McDonald's restaurants.

263
00:20:08,620 --> 00:20:13,660
By the late 60s, the fast food industry was a multi-billion dollar giant.

264
00:20:15,940 --> 00:20:23,580
McDonald's, Burger King, Jack in the Box, Carl's Jr., KFC, and many others were battling for America's fast food dollars.

265
00:20:23,620 --> 00:20:28,620
Training academies such as McDonald's Hamburger University

266
00:20:28,621 --> 00:20:31,620
schooled managers and crews in the art and science

267
00:20:31,621 --> 00:20:33,620
of fast food preparation.

268
00:20:33,621 --> 00:20:38,620
But reports from the front line trenches repeated the same demand.

269
00:20:38,621 --> 00:20:42,620
The need for speed. We have to be faster.

270
00:20:42,621 --> 00:20:47,620
You need to produce the same kind of speed of service day in and day out,

271
00:20:47,621 --> 00:20:51,620
whether it's 11 o'clock at night or 10 o'clock in the morning.

272
00:20:51,621 --> 00:20:58,200
So this bled to a great deal of interest in techniques of improving the speed of service.

273
00:21:02,200 --> 00:21:09,180
In 1969, a KFC store manager named Dave Thomas abandoned his fried chicken roots

274
00:21:09,181 --> 00:21:13,640
and opened his first Wendy's old-fashioned hamburger restaurant in Columbus, Ohio.

275
00:21:15,020 --> 00:21:17,820
A year later, a second Wendy's went up in Columbus.

276
00:21:17,821 --> 00:21:25,780
It was typical freestanding fast food architecture with one major difference, something called a pickup window.

277
00:21:27,080 --> 00:21:30,560
It had been introduced as an experiment to bolster dining room sales.

278
00:21:31,360 --> 00:21:33,780
But busy drivers discovered its higher virtue.

279
00:21:34,460 --> 00:21:36,800
You could pick up your food without leaving your car.

280
00:21:37,900 --> 00:21:45,300
Five years later, 100 Wendy's restaurants across the country were bringing in total sales of $25 million.

281
00:21:45,301 --> 00:21:46,001
dollars.

282
00:21:46,002 --> 00:21:51,200
Wendy's was the first of the nationwide chains to put drive-thru windows in all its

283
00:21:51,201 --> 00:21:54,627
restaurants. The idea wasn't exactly new.

284
00:21:54,628 --> 00:21:58,640
The first drive-thru window takes us back to the

285
00:21:58,641 --> 00:22:03,134
birthplace of the drive-in restaurant itself, the Texas Pig Stand.

286
00:22:03,135 --> 00:22:04,700
By 1931, the Pig Stands

287
00:22:04,701 --> 00:22:11,180
had spread to California. Pig Stand 21 boasted the first drive-thru service.

288
00:22:11,181 --> 00:22:17,200
The first drive-thru window that we created was an experiment.

289
00:22:17,700 --> 00:22:20,040
It was very, I guess, in a primitive form.

290
00:22:20,240 --> 00:22:24,760
There was a big sign in front of the little stand that said, drive-thru.

291
00:22:25,940 --> 00:22:29,060
And then the cook, instead of being inside the building,

292
00:22:29,680 --> 00:22:33,700
the manager or the cook would come out and deliver the order to the car.

293
00:22:34,700 --> 00:22:38,180
The first of the modern burger restaurants to offer window service

294
00:22:38,181 --> 00:22:41,000
was In-N-Out Burger of Baldwin Park, California.

295
00:22:41,180 --> 00:22:47,180
Harry and Esther Snyder opened the first In-N-Out in 1948.

296
00:22:47,181 --> 00:22:52,180
Unlike McDonald's and Burger King, the Snyders resisted franchise fever.

297
00:22:52,181 --> 00:23:00,180
And to this day, In-N-Out remains a family-owned company, with restaurants in California, Nevada, and Arizona.

298
00:23:02,180 --> 00:23:09,180
McDonald's resisted the drive-through concept until a store manager in Arizona took matters into his own hands.

299
00:23:09,181 --> 00:23:15,180
The first drive-thru McDonald's was started in 1975 in Sierra Vista, Arizona.

300
00:23:15,700 --> 00:23:21,520
One of our franchisees there saw that he had a huge opportunity in being able to serve the military folks there

301
00:23:21,521 --> 00:23:24,420
because they were unable to come into his restaurant in uniform.

302
00:23:24,900 --> 00:23:27,720
So what he did in order to capitalize on that opportunity,

303
00:23:28,300 --> 00:23:33,400
he blew a wall out of his building and made it the first drive-thru in the McDonald's system.

304
00:23:34,820 --> 00:23:38,000
That same year, Burger King offered drive-thru service.

305
00:23:38,001 --> 00:23:42,160
And from that moment on, the battle of the windows was joined.

306
00:23:42,700 --> 00:23:44,040
It totally surprised them.

307
00:23:44,200 --> 00:23:50,460
Within 10 years, virtually all of McDonald's restaurants had drive-thru windows.

308
00:23:51,220 --> 00:23:59,000
The demand for drive-thru service challenged the fast food industry to rethink and retool its operations from the ground up.

309
00:23:59,001 --> 00:24:06,300
Ready Access Pass-Through Window Systems of West Chicago, Illinois, was one of the first

310
00:24:06,301 --> 00:24:09,560
manufacturers of drive-through windows.

311
00:24:09,561 --> 00:24:12,240
We went to our first convention in 1979.

312
00:24:12,241 --> 00:24:18,200
We took the window to market and people would come by and say, well, what is that for?

313
00:24:18,201 --> 00:24:20,240
We'd tell them, it's a drive-through window.

314
00:24:20,241 --> 00:24:23,460
And they'd look at it and say, it must be a miniature model because how can you drive

315
00:24:23,461 --> 00:24:24,640
a car through that?

316
00:24:24,641 --> 00:24:29,080
So that was in 1979, and hardly anybody knew what drive-thru was.

317
00:24:29,900 --> 00:24:33,840
The first windows were simple sliding panes set in aluminum frames.

318
00:24:34,740 --> 00:24:37,260
They were usually constructed by glazing contractors.

319
00:24:38,120 --> 00:24:43,640
Then, actual drive-thru window makers like Ready Access and Quick Serve Incorporated got into the act.

320
00:24:47,660 --> 00:24:51,840
Windows would be developed to accommodate just about any form of fast food.

321
00:24:51,841 --> 00:24:56,720
But in the early days, the operators discovered that the most difficult item to move through

322
00:24:56,721 --> 00:24:59,280
a window system was information.

323
00:24:59,281 --> 00:25:05,100
Well, my family opened one of the first drive-thrus in the area in 1977.

324
00:25:05,101 --> 00:25:08,580
And at that time, the technology was pretty primitive.

325
00:25:08,581 --> 00:25:12,860
It was actually just a regular speaker that actually was positioned in the drive-thru

326
00:25:12,861 --> 00:25:17,460
booth and the person working in drive-thru had to physically stand in front of the speaker

327
00:25:17,461 --> 00:25:21,680
to listen to what the customer was ordering at the drive-thru menu board.

328
00:25:21,681 --> 00:25:25,960
Once the information got inside the restaurant, a whole new set of challenges arose.

329
00:25:26,480 --> 00:25:29,740
In the early days, we were writing things down on menu pads.

330
00:25:30,200 --> 00:25:36,000
Initially, it was a blank piece of paper, and then at one point, we moved to having all of the menu items written on that pad,

331
00:25:36,160 --> 00:25:40,720
and we could just jot down how many of each item, and then physically add it up.

332
00:25:42,260 --> 00:25:45,660
Beyond the challenges of technical hardware that delivered the fast,

333
00:25:45,840 --> 00:25:48,960
the industry had to develop a pipeline to deliver the food.

334
00:25:48,961 --> 00:25:55,060
Throughout the 80s, the burger giant set about designing a network of suppliers that

335
00:25:55,061 --> 00:26:00,240
could deliver millions of tons of food to thousands of restaurants at exacting standards

336
00:26:00,241 --> 00:26:03,780
of uniformity and do it with speed.

337
00:26:03,781 --> 00:26:08,320
It was invented by McDonald's, working with its suppliers and its vendors.

338
00:26:08,321 --> 00:26:12,580
And when McDonald's wants to do something, they call a vendor up and guess what?

339
00:26:12,581 --> 00:26:17,900
They've got 50 guys who would love to meet that need.

340
00:26:17,901 --> 00:26:23,150
specifications were sent out to meat packers, bakers, farmers and condiment makers.

341
00:26:23,151 --> 00:26:23,851
The

342
00:26:23,852 --> 00:26:28,160
message was clear. If you want to sell to us, you have to make it our way.

343
00:26:30,900 --> 00:26:36,100
Of all the products that pass through the drive-thru window, no single product was heaped

344
00:26:36,101 --> 00:26:42,141
with such lavish technical and scientific attention as the French fried potato.

345
00:26:42,142 --> 00:26:42,842
It was

346
00:26:42,843 --> 00:26:46,018
the perfect car-friendly eat-while-you-drive food.

347
00:26:46,019 --> 00:26:48,820
Americans consume an average of 30 pounds

348
00:26:48,821 --> 00:26:55,393
of fries per capita each year for a total of approximately 4 million annual tons.

349
00:26:55,394 --> 00:26:56,094
Hence,

350
00:26:56,095 --> 00:27:01,672
every phase of French fry production is monitored from soil to seasoning.

351
00:27:01,673 --> 00:27:03,180
Companies like J.R.

352
00:27:03,181 --> 00:27:09,140
Simplot and Lamb Weston in Idaho figured out how to deliver fries the way fast food giants

353
00:27:09,141 --> 00:27:09,882
wanted them.

354
00:27:09,883 --> 00:27:14,580
They built mammoth French fry factories that could turn out a million pounds

355
00:27:14,581 --> 00:27:15,927
of fries a day.

356
00:27:15,928 --> 00:27:23,020
Mountain ranges of whole potatoes are washed and sorted and sorted again. Their

357
00:27:23,021 --> 00:27:28,052
skins are blasted off in kettles of pressurized steam.

358
00:27:28,053 --> 00:27:31,220
The spuds are then fired through a

359
00:27:31,221 --> 00:27:37,100
device called a water gun knife, where a lattice of carbon steel blades sections them into

360
00:27:37,101 --> 00:27:39,442
fries of uniform thickness.

361
00:27:39,443 --> 00:27:44,820
Further downstream, another set of cutters uses laser beams to

362
00:27:44,821 --> 00:27:48,350
ensure a perfect eat-while-you-drive length.

363
00:27:48,351 --> 00:27:51,880
After several more layers of inspection, the

364
00:27:51,881 --> 00:27:57,240
fries are blanched and partially fried, then bagged and fast-frozen for their trip to a

365
00:27:57,241 --> 00:27:58,907
restaurant near you.

366
00:27:58,908 --> 00:28:04,740
And as the speed of life moved ever faster, that restaurant was moving

367
00:28:04,741 --> 00:28:10,120
more and more mountains of burgers, fries, and drinks through the hole in the wall.

368
00:28:10,121 --> 00:28:11,520
I'm fine. How are you?

369
00:28:16,000 --> 00:28:20,920
Ready Access Pass-Through Window Systems makes drive-through windows for most of the major

370
00:28:20,921 --> 00:28:22,288
fast food chains.

371
00:28:22,289 --> 00:28:28,160
In their West Chicago plant, they turn out almost 4,000 windows per year.

372
00:28:28,860 --> 00:28:31,360
They're designed to withstand a lifetime of abuse.

373
00:28:31,361 --> 00:28:39,620
Doors open 30, 40 times in a building and then the drive-through probably open 2,000 times a day.

374
00:28:39,800 --> 00:28:41,740
You know, it's just opening and closing.

375
00:28:42,020 --> 00:28:47,780
And so we have to build a product that's going to withstand because everyone operates the window a little differently.

376
00:28:50,040 --> 00:28:53,420
Raw materials are distributed to the various assembly lines.

377
00:28:53,421 --> 00:28:58,780
Each window is assembled by hand using the latest in technology and materials,

378
00:28:58,781 --> 00:29:01,460
including some from the aircraft industry.

379
00:29:01,461 --> 00:29:08,740
What we tried to do over here was to try to come up with parts and pieces in the window

380
00:29:08,741 --> 00:29:10,861
that we wouldn't have to replace.

381
00:29:10,862 --> 00:29:14,140
Like on the 275, we don't just use regular rollers,

382
00:29:14,141 --> 00:29:20,340
we use stainless steel aircraft rollers so they won't freeze or bind up in the wintertime.

383
00:29:20,341 --> 00:29:25,340
seven years we've never replaced the roller.

384
00:29:25,341 --> 00:29:32,100
The windows range in complexity from manual to semi-automatic to fully automatic.

385
00:29:32,101 --> 00:29:38,860
Once the frame is completed, the glass panes are fitted to the track mechanism.

386
00:29:38,861 --> 00:29:41,900
The final operation is testing.

387
00:29:41,901 --> 00:29:46,400
On our electric windows, we run 48-hour tests to make sure the boards burn, everything is

388
00:29:46,401 --> 00:29:48,160
sent in sync and running the right way.

389
00:29:48,580 --> 00:29:50,540
So if something happens, there's a malfunction,

390
00:29:50,760 --> 00:29:52,640
we want to get it to happen in the plant,

391
00:29:52,760 --> 00:29:53,640
not anyplace else.

392
00:29:57,060 --> 00:29:59,780
Today's drive-through window is the last stop

393
00:29:59,781 --> 00:30:02,360
in a high-tech communication and delivery system.

394
00:30:02,960 --> 00:30:04,820
We kind of break the drive-through down

395
00:30:04,821 --> 00:30:06,600
into three separate areas.

396
00:30:07,080 --> 00:30:11,060
Let me order a Happy Meal cheeseburger.

397
00:30:11,720 --> 00:30:14,160
The ordering points, which is at the menu board,

398
00:30:14,161 --> 00:30:20,900
the pay window or cashier window, and then the present window where you actually pick up the food and drive away.

399
00:30:26,800 --> 00:30:32,320
The early drive-through squawk box speaker systems were like talking through tin cans and string.

400
00:30:33,200 --> 00:30:36,760
Today's menu board microphones are cushioned by foam insulation

401
00:30:36,761 --> 00:30:43,460
and have been specially designed to filter out highs and lows and hone in on the decibel range of the human voice.

402
00:30:44,160 --> 00:30:45,160
Thank you.

403
00:30:45,161 --> 00:30:47,160
Okay, is there going to be anything else for you?

404
00:30:47,161 --> 00:30:51,160
Order confirmation boards help ensure order accuracy.

405
00:30:51,161 --> 00:30:54,160
We have perfect communication with our customer.

406
00:30:54,161 --> 00:30:57,160
We can hear them very clearly as they place the order,

407
00:30:57,161 --> 00:31:02,160
making it easier for us to serve them accurately and quickly as well.

408
00:31:02,161 --> 00:31:06,160
The crew people are wearing headsets, and they have belt packs

409
00:31:06,161 --> 00:31:08,160
where they can hear the customer ordering.

410
00:31:08,161 --> 00:31:12,160
The advantage of that is that they can begin to speak to the customer

411
00:31:12,161 --> 00:31:17,160
and take their order and initiate it without actually physically standing in drive-through.

412
00:31:17,161 --> 00:31:23,160
Computer screens flash the order to multiple stations inside the restaurant.

413
00:31:23,161 --> 00:31:31,160
The orders come up on those screens right there, and each screen is represented on one side of the preparation line.

414
00:31:31,161 --> 00:31:40,160
So before the customer is really finished ordering, most of the items are already prepared and moving down the line.

415
00:31:40,161 --> 00:31:48,320
This double lane drive-through allows for twice the volume at peak hours.

416
00:31:48,321 --> 00:31:52,780
Lane traffic is monitored by television cameras.

417
00:31:52,781 --> 00:31:57,400
Order taking time is the longest time in the entire process.

418
00:31:57,401 --> 00:32:01,780
And what we do here is we're able to order two different orders at a time.

419
00:32:01,781 --> 00:32:06,800
The order board on the far side we call the primary side and this is the secondary side.

420
00:32:06,801 --> 00:32:11,860
Once you have two or three cars waiting to order, they can come and order on either side

421
00:32:11,861 --> 00:32:16,400
and you don't have that long wait to order.

422
00:32:16,401 --> 00:32:22,920
But even the best ordering system can sometimes benefit from a little low-tech innovation.

423
00:32:22,921 --> 00:32:27,840
We put these little stickers right up here on the screen that shows the cars in the drive-through

424
00:32:27,841 --> 00:32:34,780
and we ask the order taker not to store the order until the car crosses this blue sticker

425
00:32:34,781 --> 00:32:35,780
tape.

426
00:32:35,781 --> 00:32:39,760
Even though we have all this great technology, you know, the technology works wonderful,

427
00:32:39,920 --> 00:32:43,760
but it works better when you don't store the order until after you pass this point right here.

428
00:32:45,720 --> 00:32:48,660
It all sounds great, Reggie, but is it fast?

429
00:32:49,040 --> 00:32:52,680
Man, it's blazing fast. It is really fast. It really is.

430
00:32:52,681 --> 00:32:56,080
This is the fastest drive-through ever conceived by a man.

431
00:32:58,700 --> 00:33:05,660
While Windows continued to rule, one company was teaching the old drive-in dog some new high-tech tricks,

432
00:33:05,780 --> 00:33:12,780
Its rapid rise in the market had been called by some the Sonic Boom.

433
00:33:12,781 --> 00:33:17,780
Sonic started out as the top hat drive-in during the 1950s.

434
00:33:17,781 --> 00:33:22,780
Rather than abandon the car hop concept, Sonic sought to streamline the process,

435
00:33:22,781 --> 00:33:28,780
using the same high-tech tools as the other fast food chains.

436
00:33:28,781 --> 00:33:34,780
I think people have a misconception about drive-ins, that it's slower than a drive-through lane,

437
00:33:34,781 --> 00:33:39,100
and actually, at least at Sonic, it's quite the contrary.

438
00:33:39,380 --> 00:33:42,720
Our average ticket time is about four minutes or less.

439
00:33:43,240 --> 00:33:47,600
And the beauty of a drive-in concept that I think has so much appeal

440
00:33:47,601 --> 00:33:53,860
is that the customer is the person that's empowered in a drive-in concept

441
00:33:53,861 --> 00:33:57,800
because you have your own menu, you don't have anybody sitting behind you

442
00:33:57,801 --> 00:34:01,320
impatiently waiting for you to decide what it is you want to eat.

443
00:34:01,321 --> 00:34:06,320
These high-tech car hops don't do any hopping onto running boards.

444
00:34:06,321 --> 00:34:13,320
The use of these headsets, that allows us to actually, while the car hop's on the lot,

445
00:34:13,321 --> 00:34:17,320
say, good afternoon, welcome to Sonic, may take your order.

446
00:34:17,321 --> 00:34:24,320
And so the customer knows we've heard them, we're responding to them, and that we will prepare their order.

447
00:34:24,321 --> 00:34:30,320
As competition increased, fast food chains continued to look for more speed.

448
00:34:30,321 --> 00:34:37,080
They searched for ways to control and manipulate every phase of the drive-through process and to identify the slow spots.

449
00:34:39,460 --> 00:34:48,600
Phase Research Timer Systems of Santa Ana, California, provided computerized timing tools that could break down the process to its smallest increments.

450
00:34:49,920 --> 00:34:54,340
The old saying goes, in order to manage something, you have to measure it.

451
00:34:54,341 --> 00:35:01,340
and hence the interest arose in the early days in somehow having a reliable,

452
00:35:01,341 --> 00:35:07,340
representative, accurate way of measuring the times for the cars as they were going through.

453
00:35:07,341 --> 00:35:13,340
Doing it for every car, all day long, every day of the week, every week of the year.

454
00:35:13,341 --> 00:35:20,340
As technology improved, these time measuring devices became more and more sophisticated.

455
00:35:20,341 --> 00:35:24,374
How much time at the cash transaction? How much to pick up?

456
00:35:24,375 --> 00:35:26,220
How long at the menu board?

457
00:35:26,540 --> 00:35:30,340
How long was it taking to greet the customer when they first arrived at the menu board

458
00:35:30,341 --> 00:35:33,340
before someone asked them, may I take your order?

459
00:35:35,580 --> 00:35:41,040
One of the problems they found was the same old bottleneck that had plagued the gas stations and toll roads.

460
00:35:42,640 --> 00:35:48,440
Because coins are involved, getting a wallet out, finding a purse, finding the right change,

461
00:35:48,441 --> 00:35:51,820
making the right change inside of the store for the customer,

462
00:35:52,320 --> 00:35:54,840
possibly changing a $50 or $100 bill,

463
00:35:55,360 --> 00:36:00,420
the slowdown that may occur when somebody gets change and they drop it in their lap.

464
00:36:01,620 --> 00:36:07,380
Think tank gurus and efficiency experts rack their brains for a solution to the pay window problem.

465
00:36:08,040 --> 00:36:10,380
But in the end, the answer came from the trenches.

466
00:36:11,200 --> 00:36:15,600
Actually, the light bulb for transponder technology came home

467
00:36:15,601 --> 00:36:18,440
as I was driving home from one of these McDonald's

468
00:36:18,441 --> 00:36:21,040
drive-through meetings one day, I drove on the tollway.

469
00:36:21,780 --> 00:36:24,660
And it just came to mind that, gee, it wouldn't be cool

470
00:36:24,661 --> 00:36:27,360
if you could use the same technology at McDonald's

471
00:36:27,361 --> 00:36:29,180
that I'm using on the tollway. Why not?

472
00:36:30,960 --> 00:36:33,580
Once again, it was RFID to the rescue.

473
00:36:34,500 --> 00:36:37,240
Although the new transponder technology is still being tested

474
00:36:37,241 --> 00:36:39,140
by McDonald's and other fast food chains,

475
00:36:39,680 --> 00:36:41,360
the early results are very promising.

476
00:36:41,361 --> 00:36:47,540
Our fast track, we get about 500 or so cars a day, so you'll get it quite a bit.

477
00:36:47,541 --> 00:36:49,220
How would this be on your fast track today?

478
00:36:49,580 --> 00:36:54,780
This is the device that actually is placed in the customer's window in their car.

479
00:36:55,100 --> 00:36:59,780
When the customer drives into the drive-thru lane, there's what we call a reader board

480
00:36:59,781 --> 00:37:03,720
above the drive-thru lane that picks up this radio frequency.

481
00:37:04,160 --> 00:37:06,800
So we ask, would you like to put that on your transponder today?

482
00:37:06,900 --> 00:37:08,200
How would this be on your fast track today?

483
00:37:08,420 --> 00:37:09,140
Yes, it will, please.

484
00:37:09,141 --> 00:37:17,840
If they say yes, then they simply verify the total on the customer order display with the total on their order pad.

485
00:37:18,200 --> 00:37:19,680
Is the total the same on both screens?

486
00:37:19,880 --> 00:37:20,580
Yes, it is.

487
00:37:20,620 --> 00:37:24,480
They say yes, they simply go to the pickup window and take their food away.

488
00:37:27,440 --> 00:37:31,740
Taking the food away is just what we've been doing for the last 25 years.

489
00:37:32,440 --> 00:37:36,920
In this short span of time, the drive-thru window has gone from a promotional experiment

490
00:37:36,921 --> 00:37:42,200
to a quarter-trillion-dollar business and an icon of our culture.

491
00:37:46,620 --> 00:37:50,340
Although fast food may have made the most noise, and money,

492
00:37:50,880 --> 00:37:53,520
it wasn't the only player in the drive-thru revolution.

493
00:37:54,420 --> 00:37:57,580
A diverse and sometimes unlikely assortment of industries

494
00:37:57,581 --> 00:38:02,140
had been catering to car-bound customers as far back as the 1930s.

495
00:38:04,200 --> 00:38:08,840
In 1932, the first drive-in movie theater opened in Camden, New Jersey.

496
00:38:09,800 --> 00:38:12,940
Now drivers could watch a talkie while eating in their cars.

497
00:38:16,520 --> 00:38:19,960
One spiritual spin-off of this was the drive-in church,

498
00:38:20,640 --> 00:38:26,820
where car-bound congregations could worship from behind the wheel.

499
00:38:27,580 --> 00:38:39,740
Cleanliness followed godliness with the advent of the drive-thru car wash and the drive-thru

500
00:38:39,741 --> 00:38:48,160
laundry.

501
00:38:48,161 --> 00:38:53,760
As more and more industries attempted to exploit this window of opportunity, a formula began

502
00:38:53,761 --> 00:38:54,760
to emerge.

503
00:38:54,761 --> 00:39:02,380
Drive-thru works wherever you have something where the customer wants to do it fast,

504
00:39:02,720 --> 00:39:05,580
and the vendor is currently doing it slowly.

505
00:39:06,340 --> 00:39:09,520
One industry that fit this definition was banking.

506
00:39:12,320 --> 00:39:19,800
In the late 1950s, banks began to offer drive-thru service using dedicated tellers inside drive-up booths.

507
00:39:19,801 --> 00:39:28,320
In the 1960s, they began to replace the humans with robot tellers, called ATMs, automated teller machines.

508
00:39:28,900 --> 00:39:33,640
The first full-function ATM was in Georgia.

509
00:39:34,180 --> 00:39:37,720
It was a DocuTel machine that was installed in 1968.

510
00:39:38,140 --> 00:39:41,540
Where did they install it? In a branch. It made no sense.

511
00:39:41,820 --> 00:39:44,023
They didn't even install it outside the branch.

512
00:39:44,024 --> 00:39:46,320
You had to walk in the bank for security reasons.

513
00:39:46,321 --> 00:39:52,600
But by the mid-70s, late 70s, you started to get machines that were built,

514
00:39:52,900 --> 00:39:54,420
and they were perfect for a drive-thru.

515
00:39:54,880 --> 00:39:58,460
Everything was positioned so the driver could work everything without getting out of the car.

516
00:40:00,660 --> 00:40:06,080
One institution not usually associated with speed got into the act in Las Vegas, Nevada.

517
00:40:07,300 --> 00:40:10,500
At the Little White Wedding Chapel, Charlotte Richards,

518
00:40:10,920 --> 00:40:13,260
the high priestess of drive-thru matrimony,

519
00:40:13,261 --> 00:40:18,660
has been performing these services for backseat brides and grooms for over 35 years.

520
00:40:18,780 --> 00:40:24,640
The inspiration for the drive-thru wedding was that I saw so many people that were handicapped,

521
00:40:24,740 --> 00:40:27,780
and I thought, what a nice way for them to come,

522
00:40:27,920 --> 00:40:32,420
and that way they don't have to spend a lot of pain getting in and out of their vehicles and so on.

523
00:40:34,080 --> 00:40:36,540
Not all drive-thrus are about making money.

524
00:40:37,380 --> 00:40:43,080
Drive-thru flu shot clinics and voter registration stops offer car-convenient community services.

525
00:40:43,260 --> 00:40:51,420
Today, drive-thrus are catering to a wider range of human needs than ever before, including

526
00:40:51,421 --> 00:40:59,400
death and taxes.

527
00:40:59,401 --> 00:41:04,320
Some communities are now trying to prohibit new construction of drive-thrus, citing problems

528
00:41:04,321 --> 00:41:06,500
of traffic, noise and pollution.

529
00:41:06,501 --> 00:41:10,260
But have we come too far to go back now?

530
00:41:10,261 --> 00:41:16,220
I know there's a lot of city fathers that would like to eliminate drive-thru because

531
00:41:16,221 --> 00:41:17,840
it's a hassle, it's a traffic hassle.

532
00:41:18,020 --> 00:41:19,420
I think drive-thru's here to stay.

533
00:41:22,920 --> 00:41:28,040
Ironically, the company that opened the first drive-thru window, the Texas pig stand, has

534
00:41:28,041 --> 00:41:29,380
returned to a slower pace.

535
00:41:29,381 --> 00:41:37,440
We're a full-service restaurant now, and we emphasize customer service.

536
00:41:37,441 --> 00:41:40,460
and knowing our customer.

537
00:41:41,420 --> 00:41:43,460
We have employees that have been with us for many years,

538
00:41:43,820 --> 00:41:47,000
and that creates a family kind of atmosphere.

539
00:41:47,660 --> 00:41:49,680
When a person comes in, we know them by name,

540
00:41:49,900 --> 00:41:52,260
and we know that they don't want cream in their coffee.

541
00:41:53,060 --> 00:41:56,380
And Joe's usually here at 10 o'clock, but he's late today.

542
00:41:56,420 --> 00:41:57,740
I wonder if he's okay.

543
00:41:57,741 --> 00:41:59,340
You know, this is what we're about.

544
00:42:01,020 --> 00:42:04,060
But in spite of Richard Haley's noble retro vision,

545
00:42:04,600 --> 00:42:06,060
the Fast Lane is here to stay.

546
00:42:06,560 --> 00:42:09,760
It is up to us to choose the speed at which we will live.

547
00:42:10,400 --> 00:42:12,100
So tonight, what do you say?

548
00:42:12,620 --> 00:42:16,060
We leave the car in the garage, open a nice bottle of wine,

549
00:42:16,360 --> 00:42:19,320
and sit down to a nice home-cooked dinner,

550
00:42:20,080 --> 00:42:22,940
slowly savoring the good things in life.

551
00:42:26,900 --> 00:42:27,900
Are you kidding?

552
00:42:28,380 --> 00:42:29,320
No way it's time for that.

553
00:42:29,380 --> 00:42:31,320
I'll just grab a burger on the way to pick up my laundry,

554
00:42:31,420 --> 00:42:34,420
get gas, hit the bank, get a flu shot, register to vote, stop, car watch, ooh, get married,

555
00:42:34,421 --> 00:42:36,120
Grab a coffee, pay my tax pay, it's all pay my respects,

556
00:42:36,121 --> 00:42:37,380
do my recycling, go to confession, pick up the numbers,

557
00:42:37,381 --> 00:42:38,660
eat, toy, and bag, raise, get big mammals,

558
00:42:38,661 --> 00:42:40,660
pick up beer, get cigars, get big cigars.

559
00:42:40,661 --> 00:42:42,660
Hi, may I take your order, please?

560
00:44:03,200 --> 00:44:04,400
Oh, my God.

